# Cloudflare Pages / Netlify header rules

# ── Security ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A static site has no database, no login, no server-side code — so the
# attacks that matter here are content injection, clickjacking, and
# downgrade-to-HTTP. These headers close those off.
#
# Content-Security-Policy is the important one: it names every place the
# browser is permitted to load anything from, and refuses the rest. If
# someone ever managed to inject a <script> into the HTML, it would not
# execute, because it wouldn't match the hash below.
#
# CAREFUL: the 'sha256-...' value is the hash of the one inline script in
# index.html:
#     document.documentElement.classList.add("js");
# Change that line by even a character and the browser will refuse to run
# it — which would leave every scroll-reveal element invisible. Recompute
# with:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update(process.argv[1]).digest('base64'))" 'SCRIPT TEXT HERE'
#
# HSTS starts at one day on purpose. It tells browsers to refuse plain HTTP
# for this domain, which is good — but they cache that instruction, so if a
# certificate ever broke, the site would be unreachable for the full duration
# with no way to click past. One day means a bad day, not a bad year. Once
# it's been running happily for a few weeks, raise it to 31536000. Only add
# `preload` if you're certain, because that one is effectively permanent.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; script-src 'self' 'sha256-WZRJfWvsnNCPcxzZwvyhovnZGqhZaC+8gPGPRbx6wTk='; connect-src 'self' https://formspree.io; form-action https://formspree.io; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), midi=(), magnetometer=(), accelerometer=(), gyroscope=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=86400

# ── While you're still building ──────────────────────────────
# Short caches, so a photo or a CSS tweak shows up on the next reload
# instead of days later. Costs a little speed, saves a lot of confusion.
#
# Three.js lives at /vendor/, NOT /js/vendor/, and that placement is load
# bearing. Cloudflare applies every matching rule and concatenates them rather
# than picking the most specific, so `/js/*` and `/js/vendor/*` would both hit
# the Three.js files and emit two conflicting max-age values. Keeping the
# vendored library outside /js/ lets one wildcard cover every script we write —
# including ones added later, which is how camera3d.js ended up with no rule at
# all and served two different versions from the edge at the same time.

/photos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

# A pinned version that never changes — cache it hard.
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable


# ── At launch ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Once the photos and copy are settled, replace the three blocks above
# (keep the vendor one as-is) with these. Repeat visits get much faster.
#
# /photos/*
#   Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# /css/*
#   Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
#
# /js/*
#   Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
